20813]]}, {"t": " certain", "lp": -0.077235646545887, "top": [[" certain", -0.077235646545887], [" confident", -2.603522777557373]]}, {"t": " about", "lp": -0.5409443378448486, "top": [[" about", -0.5409443378448486], [" of", -0.9182770848274231]]}, {"t": " the", "lp": -0.19495080411434174, "top": [[" the", -0.19495080411434174], [" every", -1.8221461772918701]]}, {"t": " exact", "lp": -0.07856865227222443, "top": [[" exact", -0.07856865227222443], [" precise", -3.0004074573516846]]}, {"t": " semantics", "lp": -1.1643931865692139, "top": [[" semantics", -1.1643931865692139], [" behavioral", -1.5276916027069092]]}, {"t": " of", "lp": -0.23220744729042053, "top": [[" of", -0.23220744729042053], [" and", -2.4103424549102783]]}], "text": "# CCS Mode on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how **Compute and Copy Engine (CCS)** worker threads are assigned to CPU cores. This is relevant because the Xe GPU relies on CPU-side \"worker\" threads to submit work to the hardware queues.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**`ccs_mode=1`** \u2014 *Auto-detect / default*\n- The driver automatically determines the CCS core assignment based on your CPU topology.\n- This is generally the recommended setting for most users, as it uses heuristics to pick a sensible mapping.\n\n**`ccs_mode=2`** \u2014 *Fixed/permanent CCS assignment*\n- CCS cores are pinned to specific CPU cores and remain assigned for the lifetime of the system (or until reboot).\n- This gives more deterministic behavior but less flexibility.\n\n## When to Use Mode 4\n\n**`ccs_mode=4`** is the **manual** mode. You'd use this when:\n\n- You want **explicit control** over exactly which CPU cores host the CCS worker threads\n- You're tuning for a **specific workload** and know which cores should be dedicated to GPU worker submission\n- You're running a **server or specialized setup** where CPU affinity matters (e.g., isolating GPU workers from other CPU-intensive processes)\n\nIn manual mode, you typically combine it with parameters specifying the core list, giving you precise affinity control.\n\n## Practical Guidance\n\nFor most users, **`ccs_mode=1` (auto)** is the best choice\u2014it \"just works\" and adapts to your hardware. Reach for **mode 4** only if you're doing performance tuning or have a specific reason to pin CCS workers to particular cores.\n\n---\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f **A note of caution:** I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of"}